.policy "ruff-up006-non-pep585-annotation" revision 2
.description "Use built-in and collections generics such as list[int] instead of typing aliases such as List[int]; the aliases are deprecated since Python 3.9."
.language python
.mode steer
.scope include "**/*"

// Ruff UP006 (pyupgrade): `typing.List` and its kin where the builtin generic works: `list[int]` (PEP 585).
// Every use counts, subscripted or not, in annotations and at runtime: from Python 3.9 on, in a stub always,
// and from 3.7 in annotations under `from __future__ import annotations` (python/upgrade-typing's rewritable).
// The table: List, Dict, Set, FrozenSet, Tuple, Type, and Deque and DefaultDict (to collections).
//   def f(x: List[str]): ...         ← UP006 at List (3.9+)
//   x: typing.DefaultDict[str, int]  ← UP006 at typing.DefaultDict
//   def f(x: "List[str]"): ...       ← UP006 inside the string, through `parsed`
#include "python/upgrade-typing.rr"
//   typing_ref(e, x), rewritable(n, m), pep585(x, b)

.violation UP006(at: node, b: symbol) code "UP006"
    "Replace `{at}` with the builtin generic `{b}`, and drop the typing import if unused."
    suppress ruff "UP006"
UP006(e, b) :- typing_ref(e, x), pep585(x, b), (ast_name(e, _) ; kind(e, "attribute")), rewritable(e, 9).
